            <ArticleTitle>Competence Acquisition Using Simulated Learning Experiences: A Concept Analysis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>102-107</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.5480/13-1198</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To report the findings of an analysis of the concept of competence acquisition when determined using simulated learning experiences.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Competence of nursing students prior to entry into practice has been stressed by the Institute of Medicine. Competence can be evaluated via simulation; however, evaluation practices vary among schools of nursing. The link between competence acquisition in simulation and clinical competence has not been determined.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHOD" NlmCategory="METHODS">Employing the methodology of , articles included in this analysis were published in english in peer-reviewed journals from 2002 to 2012 and contained information on simulation outcomes related to competence. Thirty-five articles and one text were included in the final analysis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Essential elements of competence acquisition identified included authentic environment, demonstration, and evaluation.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Use of a consistent language and framework to evaluate competence acquisition in simulation is recommended, as is future research to test the constructs.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
